## Solution

We work in the category **Ab** of abelian groups. Consider the inverse system indexed by ℕ with objects $A_i = \mathbb{Q}$ for all $i \ge 0$ and bonding maps $f_i : A_{i+1} \to A_i$ given by $f_i(x) = n\cdot x$ (multiplication by the fixed integer $n>1$). The **sequential (inverse) limit** is

$$\varprojlim A_i \;=\; \bigl\{(a_0, a_1, a_2, \dots) \in \prod_{i\ge 0}\mathbb{Q} \;\big|\; n\,a_{i+1}=a_i \text{ for all } i\ge 0\bigr\}.$$

### Claim

$\varprojlim A_i \cong \mathbb{Q}$ (as abelian groups), the isomorphism being projection onto the $0$-th coordinate.

### Proof

**The projection is an isomorphism.** Let $\pi_0 : \varprojlim A_i \to \mathbb{Q}$ be $\pi_0\bigl((a_i)\bigr)=a_0$.

*Injectivity.* If $\pi_0\bigl((a_i)\bigr)=0$, then $a_0=0$. The compatibility relation gives $a_i = a_0/n^i = 0$ for every $i$, so $(a_i)=0$.

*Surjectivity.* Given any $q\in\mathbb{Q}$, set $a_i = q/n^i \in \mathbb{Q}$. This is well-defined because $\mathbb{Q}$ is a **divisible** group: division by $n^i$ is always possible in $\mathbb{Q}$. Then $n\,a_{i+1} = n\cdot(q/n^{i+1}) = q/n^i = a_i$, so $(a_i)\in\varprojlim A_i$ and $\pi_0\bigl((a_i)\bigr)=q$.

Hence $\pi_0$ is a group isomorphism, and $\varprojlim A_i \cong \mathbb{Q}$.

### Why divisibility is essential

The key property used is that $\mathbb{Q}$ is **divisible**: multiplication by $n$ is *surjective* on $\mathbb{Q}$. This guarantees that every $a_0$ can be "lifted" indefinitely through the tower, producing a unique compatible thread. (Contrast with $\mathbb{Z}$, where multiplication by $n$ is not surjective; there the same inverse limit would be trivial, since an integer divisible by $n^k$ for all $k$ must be $0$.)

### Universal property verification

For any abelian group $B$ with a compatible family of maps $g_i : B \to \mathbb{Q}$ satisfying $n\,g_{i+1}=g_i$, the unique map $g:B\to\varprojlim A_i$ induced by the universal property corresponds, under the isomorphism above, to $g_0:B\to\mathbb{Q}$. Indeed $g_i = g_0/n^i$ is forced by compatibility, so $g_0$ determines the entire family, confirming the universal property is satisfied by $\mathbb{Q}$ with the identity-compatible projections.

### Conclusion

The categorical sequential limit of the tower $\cdots \xrightarrow{\times n} \mathbb{Q} \xrightarrow{\times n} \mathbb{Q} \xrightarrow{\times n} \mathbb{Q}$ in **Ab** is

$$\boxed{\varprojlim \;\cong\; \mathbb{Q}},$$

with the projection onto any coordinate giving the isomorphism.

